---
// A before-and-after pair of short flows, freshly drawn and generic: how a
// stalled job comes to light under a reactive set-up and under a proactive one.
// Labelled as illustrative. It is not a screen, and nothing in it is taken from
// any real system.
//
// Real HTML lists, so the text is read as it is and axe can check its contrast.
// The connectors between steps are drawn in CSS (illustrations.css) and carry no
// text. "Before" is dashed and quiet, "After" is solid and in the accent, so the
// difference does not depend on colour alone. Side by side from 40rem, stacked
// below that.
const before = ['A job stalls', 'Nobody knows', 'The customer chases', 'Customer service goes digging'];
const after = ['A job stalls', 'The system alerts customer service', 'They act before the customer asks'];
---

<figure class="flows">
  <p class="illus-tag">Illustrative</p>
  <div class="flows__grid">
    <div class="flow flow--before" role="group" aria-labelledby="flow-before">
      <p class="flow__title" id="flow-before">Before: reactive</p>
      <ol class="flow__steps">
        {before.map((step) => <li class="flow__step">{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="flow flow--after" role="group" aria-labelledby="flow-after">
      <p class="flow__title" id="flow-after">After: proactive</p>
      <ol class="flow__steps">
        {after.map((step) => <li class="flow__step">{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="illus-caption">
    The same stalled job, handled two ways. In the first, the customer finds out before we do. In the second, we do.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
